  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM MDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nAn active pattern setting up for much of the next week. A \nsouthwest to southerly flow pattern persists at least into Sunday.\nThis afternoon and evening, we will see a series of disturbances \nmoving through the region which will help trigger scattered \nthunderstorms, primarily west of the RGV, but a few could move \ninto El Paso and parts of Otero county. There is some good shear \nout west with 35-45KTS in the 0-6km layer and CAPE values upwards \nof 1000J/kg. Could see a few strong to low end severe. It does \nlook like it will be more low-topped storms, but with cooler Oct \nair in place, hail will be a threat as well as wind. This activity\nwill move NE through the evening. Overnight lows will be warmer \nthan last night with higher dew points in place. \n\nTue looks like a more down day as we will see some ridging taking\nplace over the eastern zones and a strong upper low moves slowly \nsouth off the CA coast, but still enough moisture and instability \naround to get a few storms over area mountains. Temperatures will \nbe warming up into the upper 70s to mid 80s for the lowlands. \nSimilar conditions continue through the day Wed except as we get \ntowards 00Z, there will be some convection developing south of the\nBootheel and pushing north into western CWA. SPC has highlighted \nthis area with a marginal severe threat. Shear will definitely be \nin place but instability looks marginal. \n\nActivity starts really ramping up going into Thu and Fri as the \nupper low moves onshore, opens up and gets into western MT. The \ntrough will extend down into the Baja Peninsula and keep a good \nmoisture fetch from the south and a series of disturbances lining \nup. Could see some training through this timeframe and locally \nheavy rain. Currently both the GFS and EC Ensemble means support \n1-2\" over the Gila region with a third to 1\" over the lowlands. \nLocalized areas could see 2-3\" by Sat morning. \n\nModels are in good agreement through Sat morning, but then differ\non how to handle the lingering upper trough. EC continues to be \nmore progressive and pushing the upper trough east of the area by \nSun morning. GFS has a different solution each run but does want \nto keep redeveloping a trough/upper low near the Four Corners \nregion and precipitation going. The jet pattern seems to favor a \nEC solution that is more progressive, but lingering moisture could\nkeep some isolated to scattered storms over the east going into \nSun. Temperatures as we get into Fri-Sun will cool down with the \ntrough passage.",
